course: dictionary
lesson: 2034
title: When things get confused
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: To confuse someone is to make them unable to understand or think clearly. «The instructions confused me.» When the steps are unclear, your head goes fuzzy and you cannot tell what to do next.
explain: To confuse two things is to mix them up and think one is the other. «I always confuse the twins.» Their faces look alike, so you pick the wrong name. Some pairs are easily confused, like their, there and they're.
explain: Use context to check the meaning. «Tricks that confuse grown-ups about who did it» is about tricks that leave grown-ups unsure. Swap in "make unclear" and read the sentence again. If it still makes sense, your meaning is right.
fact: To confuse means to make someone unable to think clearly, or to mix two things up.
fact: «Confuse» can also mean to make a situation more complicated or unclear.
fact: «Confuse A with B» means you think A is B, as in «I always confuse the twins».
fact: «Cuttlefish» can change their skin patterns, which «may confuse prey».
fact: Confuses, confused and confusing are other forms of the same word.
exercise: «The instructions confused me.» What does confused mean here? | Put in the right order | *Unable to understand or think clearly | Full of joy | Very tired from running
exercise: «I always confuse the twins.» What does confuse mean here? | To make them laugh | *To mix them up and think one is the other | To ask them a question | To hug them
exercise: Which pair of words do children often confuse at school? | *their and there | red and blue | happy and sad | big and tall
test: «Tricks that confuse grown-ups about who did it often end with everyone sharing the punishment.» In this sentence, confuse means what? | To make grown-ups laugh | To help grown-ups find the answer quickly | To give grown-ups a prize | *To make grown-ups unsure of the truth
test: Which phrase shows you have mixed two things up? | Shout A at B | Tidy up A and B | *Confuse A with B | Count A to B
exam: Which sentence shows a situation made more unclear? | The team in red won the match | We ate our lunch outside today | *The new rules made the game even more confusing for everyone | Everyone cheered when the bell rang
